# Solution — GIAM 7.3 Problem 7

## Problem

Prove that in a simple graph (an undirected graph with no loops or parallel edges) having $ n $ nodes, there must be two nodes having the same degree.

(Implicit: $ n \ge 2 $, since "two nodes" requires at least two vertices. We return to the $ n = 1 $ edge case below.)

## Pigeonhole Framing — and Why the Naive Version Fails

Let $ G = (V, E) $ be a simple graph with $ |V| = n \ge 2 $. For each $ v \in V $, the *degree* $ \deg(v) $ is the number of edges of $ G $ incident to $ v $. Since $ G $ has no loops and no parallel edges, $ v $ is adjacent to at most one of each of the other $ n - 1 $ vertices, so

$$$
\deg(v) \;\in\; \{0,\, 1,\, 2,\, \ldots,\, n - 1\}.
$$$

The degree map is then a function

$$$
d \;\colon\; V \;\longrightarrow\; \{0,\, 1,\, \ldots,\, n - 1\}.
$$$

**A naive attempt.** The domain $ V $ has size $ n $; the codomain $ \{0, 1, \ldots, n-1\} $ also has size $ n $. So Theorem $ 7.3.1 $ does **not** apply directly — $ n > n $ is false. We need a sharper observation to shrink the effective codomain.

## Step 1 — The Endpoints $ 0 $ and $ n - 1 $ Cannot Both Occur

**Claim.** In any simple graph on $ n \ge 2 $ vertices, the image $ d(V) $ contains *at most one* of the values $ 0 $ and $ n - 1 $.

*Proof.* Suppose both $ 0 \in d(V) $ and $ n - 1 \in d(V) $. Pick $ u, v \in V $ with $ d(u) = 0 $ and $ d(v) = n - 1 $. Note $ u \ne v $ since $ d(u) \ne d(v) $.

Since $ d(v) = n - 1 $, vertex $ v $ is adjacent to *every* other vertex of $ G $ — in particular, $ v $ is adjacent to $ u $. But then the edge $ \{u, v\} $ is incident to $ u $, so $ d(u) \ge 1 $, contradicting $ d(u) = 0 $.

Hence $ \{0, n - 1\} \not\subseteq d(V) $. $ \square $

!(85%)[A vertex of degree n-1 forces every other vertex to have degree ≥ 1, ruling out degree 0](problem-7-contradiction.svg "A deg-(n-1) vertex is adjacent to every other vertex. If a deg-0 (isolated) vertex existed, the edge between them would have to exist (forced from the deg-(n-1) side) and cannot exist (forbidden from the deg-0 side) — a contradiction.")

## Step 2 — The Effective Codomain Has Size $ \le n - 1 $

By Step $ 1 $, $ d(V) $ misses at least one of $ \{0, n - 1\} $, so

$$$
d(V) \;\subseteq\; \begin{cases} \{0,\, 1,\, \ldots,\, n - 2\} & \text{if } n - 1 \notin d(V), \\ \{1,\, 2,\, \ldots,\, n - 1\} & \text{if } 0 \notin d(V). \end{cases}
$$$

Both sets have size $ n - 1 $. So $ |d(V)| \le n - 1 $.

## Step 3 — Apply the Pigeonhole Principle

Replace the codomain of $ d $ by the image to get the surjection

$$$
d \;\colon\; V \;\twoheadrightarrow\; d(V), \qquad |V| = n, \quad |d(V)| \le n - 1.
$$$

Since $ n > n - 1 $, Theorem $ 7.3.1 $ tells us $ d $ is not injective: there exist distinct $ u, v \in V $ with

$$$
\deg(u) \;=\; \deg(v).
$$$

That is, *two vertices of $ G $ share a degree.* $ \blacksquare $

## A Worked Example — $ n = 4 $

The picture below shows a simple graph on $ V = \{A, B, C, D\} $ with edges

$$$
E \;=\; \{\, AB,\; AC,\; AD,\; BC \,\}.
$$$

Computing degrees:

| Vertex | Incident edges     | Degree |
| :---:  | :---:              | :---:  |
| $ A $  | $ AB, AC, AD $     | $ 3 $  |
| $ B $  | $ AB, BC $         | $ 2 $  |
| $ C $  | $ AC, BC $         | $ 2 $  |
| $ D $  | $ AD $             | $ 1 $  |

Degree multiset: $ \{1, 2, 2, 3\} $. Possible degrees in $ \{0, 1, 2, 3\} $ ($ = \{0, 1, \ldots, n - 1\} $ for $ n = 4 $) — but $ 0 \notin d(V) $ since $ A $ has degree $ n - 1 = 3 $, forcing every other vertex to have degree $ \ge 1 $. The image $ d(V) = \{1, 2, 3\} $ has size $ 3 = n - 1 $, and the four vertices map into it — pigeonhole forces a collision. Here $ B $ and $ C $ both have degree $ 2 $.

!(60%)[A simple graph on 4 vertices with degrees 1, 2, 2, 3; the two degree-2 vertices are highlighted](problem-7-example.svg "A 4-vertex simple graph with edge set {AB, AC, AD, BC}. Vertices are labeled by name (A, B, C, D) with degree numbers inside the circles. Vertices B and C share degree 2 (highlighted in red); A has degree 3 and D has degree 1.")

## The Edge Case $ n = 1 $

For $ n = 1 $, the graph has a single vertex of degree $ 0 $. The statement "two nodes have the same degree" is vacuously *false* (there are not even two nodes). The original claim is implicitly about $ n \ge 2 $, and the proof above operates in that regime.

(For $ n = 2 $, the proof goes through: possible degrees in $ \{0, 1\} $, but both cannot coexist — a degree-$ 1 $ vertex needs an edge to *some* other vertex, which would not be isolated. So either both vertices have degree $ 0 $ or both have degree $ 1 $, and they share. $ \checkmark $)

## Remark — Why "Simple" is Essential

The proof uses the hypothesis "no loops, no parallel edges" via the upper bound $ \deg(v) \le n - 1 $. Dropping either assumption breaks the argument — and the conclusion.

**With parallel edges (multigraph).** Take $ n = 3 $ with vertices $ \{A, B, C\} $, one edge $ AB $, and two edges $ BC $. Degrees: $ A: 1, B: 3, C: 2 $. All distinct. ✗

**With loops.** Take $ n = 3 $ with $ \{A, B, C\} $, a loop on $ A $, and an edge $ AC $. Each loop contributes $ 2 $ to its vertex's degree. Degrees: $ A: 2 + 1 = 3, B: 0, C: 1 $. All distinct. ✗

So both restrictions are *individually* necessary for the conclusion to hold.

## Remark — Tightness

The bound "*at least two* vertices share a degree" cannot be sharpened to "*at least three* share a degree" in general. There exist simple graphs on $ n \ge 2 $ vertices where the degree multiset has exactly one repeated value (occurring twice) and all other degrees are distinct.

Example for $ n = 4 $: the graph above has degree multiset $ \{1, 2, 2, 3\} $ — only the value $ 2 $ is repeated (twice).

Example for $ n = 5 $: take the graph with edges $ \{AB, AC, AD, AE, BC, CE\} $. Degrees: $ A: 4, B: 2, C: 3, D: 1, E: 2 $. Multiset $ \{1, 2, 2, 3, 4\} $ — only $ 2 $ repeated, exactly twice.

In each case the pigeonhole bound is *exactly* met: $ n $ vertices into $ n - 1 $ possible degree values, with exactly one collision.

## Remark — Alternative Framing via Pairing

The argument can be restated more symmetrically. Pair the possible degree values

$$$
0 \;\longleftrightarrow\; n - 1, \qquad 1 \;\longleftrightarrow\; n - 2, \qquad \ldots
$$$

into pairs $ \{k, n - 1 - k\} $. Each pair represents a "vertex and its non-neighbors": if $ \deg(v) = k $ then $ v $ is *non*-adjacent to $ n - 1 - k $ other vertices.

In a simple graph $ G $, the *complement graph* $ \bar G $ has $ \deg_{\bar G}(v) = n - 1 - \deg_G(v) $. So the pairing above swaps the degree functions $ d_G $ and $ d_{\bar G} $. The non-coexistence of $ 0 $ and $ n - 1 $ becomes: $ G $ cannot have a "universal" vertex and an "isolated" vertex simultaneously — equivalently, $ \bar G $ cannot have the same.

This complement perspective also gives a strengthening: *both* the graph $ G $ and its complement $ \bar G $ must have two vertices of the same degree, but the *pairs* of equal-degree vertices may differ between $ G $ and $ \bar G $.

## Remark — A Stronger Statement

A counting-mod-$ 2 $ argument (Problem $ 2 $ in Section $ 7.2 $) gives:

> *In any simple graph, the number of odd-degree vertices is even.*

This is *not* the statement we proved here (which is about repeated degree *values*), but it shows the same theme: degree sequences in simple graphs are *constrained* in non-obvious ways. Other classical constraints:

- **Erdős–Gallai theorem.** Characterizes which sequences $ (d_1, \ldots, d_n) $ arise as degree sequences of *some* simple graph.
- **Havel–Hakimi algorithm.** Constructive realization test for proposed degree sequences.

Our pigeonhole observation is the simplest such constraint: *no simple graph realizes the degree sequence* $ (0, 1, 2, \ldots, n - 1) $. Equivalently, the all-distinct degree sequence is impossible.
